## Comparison with Non-RK methods

Now let's test Tsit5 and Vern9 against parallel extrapolation methods and an
Adams-Bashforth-Moulton:

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>Tsit5())
          Dict(:alg=>Vern9())
          Dict(:alg=>VCABM())
          Dict(:alg=>IRKGL16(simd = false))
          Dict(:alg=>IRKGL16(simd = true))
          Dict(:alg=>AitkenNeville(min_order = 1, max_order = 9, init_order = 4, threading = true))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointDeuflhard(
              min_order = 1, max_order = 9, init_order = 4, threading = true))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4, threading = true))]
solnames = ["Tsit5", "Vern9", "VCABM", "IRKGL16", "IRKGL16 SIMD",
    "AitkenNeville", "Midpoint Deuflhard", "Midpoint Hairer Wanner"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; appxsol = test_sol, names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
plot(wp)
```

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointDeuflhard(
              min_order = 1, max_order = 9, init_order = 9, threading = false))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4, threading = false))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4, threading = true))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4,
              sequence = :romberg, threading = true))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4,
              sequence = :bulirsch, threading = true))]
solnames = ["Deuflhard", "No threads", "standard", "Romberg", "Bulirsch"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; appxsol = test_sol, names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
plot(wp)
```

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 10, threading = true))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 11, init_order = 4, threading = true))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 5, max_order = 11, init_order = 10, threading = true))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 2, max_order = 15, init_order = 10, threading = true))
          Dict(:alg=>ExtrapolationMidpointHairerWanner(
              min_order = 5, max_order = 7, init_order = 6, threading = true))]
solnames = ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; appxsol = test_sol, names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
plot(wp)
```

## Spectral Deferred Correction

`SDC` from OrdinaryDiffEqSDC iterates a cheap sweeper over the nodes of a
collocation problem instead of applying a fixed tableau. With the explicit-Euler
sweeper `SDCSweeper.FE`, each node update is one explicit Euler step between
adjacent nodes plus a correction integral, so a step on `M` nodes with `K`
sweeps costs about `K*M` right-hand-side evaluations. Each sweep raises the
order by about one until the collocation order is reached, so `num_sweeps`
rather than a new tableau is what changes the accuracy. On the default
Radau-right Legendre nodes `num_nodes = 4` has collocation order 7, so
`num_sweeps = 4` and `num_sweeps = 7` give adaptive methods of order roughly 4
and 7 on the same nodes; the embedded estimate is the difference between the
updates formed by the last two sweeps.

```julia
setups = [Dict(:alg=>Tsit5())
          Dict(:alg=>Vern9())
          Dict(:alg=>SDC(sweeper = SDCSweeper.FE, num_nodes = 4, num_sweeps = 4))
          Dict(:alg=>SDC(sweeper = SDCSweeper.FE, num_nodes = 4, num_sweeps = 7))]
solnames = ["Tsit5", "Vern9", "SDC-FE (4 nodes, 4 sweeps)", "SDC-FE (4 nodes, 7 sweeps)"]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(prob, abstols, reltols, setups; appxsol = test_sol, names = solnames,
    save_everystep = false, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(), numruns = 100)
plot(wp)
```

How many right-hand-side evaluations does each sweep count spend, and are any of
the plotted points only reporting the accuracy of the reference trajectory? The
reference is bounded against a tighter solve first; an achieved error within an
order of magnitude of that gap is marked reference-limited.

```julia
using Statistics
tighter_ref = solve(prob, Vern9(), abstol = 1 / 10^14, reltol = 1 / 10^12,
    maxiters = 1000000)
ref_floor = mean(abs.(sol.u[end] - tighter_ref.u[end]))
println("Final-point gap between the reference and a tighter solve: ", ref_floor)
for (name, setup) in zip(solnames[3:4], setups[3:4])
    for (abstol, reltol) in zip(abstols, reltols)
        s = solve(prob, setup[:alg]; abstol = abstol, reltol = reltol)
        err = mean(abs.(s.u[end] - test_sol(s.t[end])))
        flag = err <= 10 * ref_floor ? " (reference-limited)" : ""
        println("$name abstol=$abstol reltol=$reltol: nf=$(s.stats.nf), final error=$err$flag")
    end
end
```

The 7-sweep method takes far fewer steps at each tolerance, but each step costs
about 1.75× more evaluations, so the two curves nearly coincide on the
error-against-work plane: around a 2e-3–3e-3 final error both sweep counts
spend roughly 2400 evaluations and about the same time, which is the one place
the extra sweeps recover their per-step cost. Elsewhere they do not. At the
loosest tolerance both spend about 1700 evaluations yet the 4-sweep run is
about twenty times more accurate, and at the tight end the 7-sweep runs never
get below a 1e-3 final error — the sweep-difference error estimate grows more
optimistic as the order rises, landing hundreds of times above the requested
tolerance — while the 4-sweep method continues to about 2e-5. No point is
reference-limited: the reference trajectory agrees with a tighter solve to
about 5e-10 in the same mean-absolute final error, more than four orders of
magnitude below the smallest error any method reaches here. Both explicit SDC variants sit above the Tsit5 and Vern9 lines at
every accuracy level — the repeated first-order sweeps spend more evaluations
per unit of accuracy than a purpose-built high-order Runge–Kutta step on this
smooth non-stiff problem.

## Conclusion

The OrdinaryDiffEq.jl algorithms come out as faster in each case than the ODEInterface algorithms. Overall, the Verner methods have a really good showing once again. The `CVODE_Adams` method does really well here when the tolerances are higher.

